# team/unix — native ORM materializer, A/B

A small harness (`bench/orm/materialize_bench.py`) reads the same 100k rows
through stock SQLAlchemy ORM and through the native materializer. The script is
mounted into both images at the same path, so baseline and change run the exact
same file; only the image tag and `BENCH_GIT_REV` differ. On the baseline image
`fastpostgres.orm` does not exist, so the script reports the stock ORM cell and
the materializer as unavailable.

## Command

```
flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e BENCH_GIT_REV=<rev> -v /home/carlos/.local/share/opencode/worktree/b2743c/team-unix/bench/orm/materialize_bench.py:/tmp/materialize_bench.py:ro fpq-<image> python3 /tmp/materialize_bench.py 100000 3'
```

## Baseline — fpq-baseline (main@7e3d2e6), BENCH_GIT_REV=baseline

```
### ENV
cpu: AMD Ryzen 7 7700 8-Core Processor
mem: 30 GiB
ghz: 3.8
cores: 8
postgres: 18.6
python: 3.13.5
sqlalchemy: 2.0.40
pyarrow: 26.0.0
adbc: 1.13.0
shape: bench
git: baseline
shm: 3.0G
### END ENV

### ORM MATERIALIZE
rows: 100000  reps: 3  shape: bench
engine: postgresql+fastpostgres://postgres@/postgres?host=/tmp
driver tuples            209.2 ns/row     4.78M rows/s
stock ORM               3361.0 ns/row     0.30M rows/s
materialize:        unavailable (No module named 'fastpostgres.orm')
### END ORM MATERIALIZE
read_rows: 631.9 cycles/row over 900001 rows (decode 630.6, frame+desc+list 1.3)
```

## Change — fpq-unix (team/unix), BENCH_GIT_REV=unix

```
### ENV
cpu: AMD Ryzen 7 7700 8-Core Processor
mem: 30 GiB
ghz: 3.8
cores: 8
postgres: 18.6
python: 3.13.5
sqlalchemy: 2.0.40
pyarrow: 26.0.0
adbc: 1.13.0
shape: bench
git: unix
shm: 3.0G
### END ENV

### ORM MATERIALIZE
rows: 100000  reps: 3  shape: bench
engine: postgresql+fastpostgres://postgres@/postgres?host=/tmp
driver tuples            215.4 ns/row     4.64M rows/s
stock ORM               3293.5 ns/row     0.30M rows/s
materialize e2e          421.3 ns/row     2.37M rows/s
materialize only         168.9 ns/row     5.92M rows/s
speedup e2e:        7.82x  entity-only: 19.50x
### END ORM MATERIALIZE
read_rows: 626.1 cycles/row over 1500001 rows (decode 624.8, frame+desc+list 1.3)
```

### AB: stock ORM 3361.0 -> materialize e2e 421.3 ns/row (-87.5%), 0.30M -> 2.37M rows/s (+7.98x); the fill alone is 168.9 ns/row (19.9x the stock ORM row), and against the same driver read (215.4 ns/row) SQLAlchemy's ORM share is ~3.1 us/row. The stock ORM control moved 3361.0 -> 3293.5 ns/row (-2.0%) between runs; a second pair was 3362.3 -> 3471.7 (+3.3%), materialize e2e 421.4 ns/row.
